# Cline Rules - Momentum Energy Dashboard

> NOTE: `AGENTS.md` is the canonical agent guide for this repo and is
> kept up to date; this file is retained for Cline compatibility. When the
> two disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins.

Project: Momentum Energy Dashboard, a Flutter app that visualises a customer''s
Momentum Energy (Australia) electricity usage from a downloaded CSV export.

## Commands
- `flutter pub get` - install/refresh dependencies.
- `flutter test` - run the test suite (~48 tests: aggregation logic, CSV
  state, and real widget suites for every screen).
- `flutter analyze` - static analysis. The baseline is 29 pre-existing
  SDK deprecation/info warnings; treat any NEW error/warning as a regression.

## Architecture
- `CsvState` (`lib/state/csv_state.dart`, a `ChangeNotifier` behind
  `provider`) parses the CSV once; `HomeShell` hosts the Data / Days / Weeks
  tabs plus the Settings and DayDetail routes. Screens never parse - they
  pass `state.rows` to `DataAggregator` for bars and to `windowTotals`
  (`lib/state/day_math.dart`) for the headline numbers, so printed totals
  are by construction the sum of the drawn bars.
- Two failure surfaces: `status == error` (+ `errorMessage`) means NOTHING
  can be drawn (`rows` empty, first load failed) and the shell shows
  onboarding; `importError` means a later import failed while a good file is
  still loaded, so `rows`/dates/`fileName` keep describing that file, the
  tabs keep drawing it, and the shell shows a dismissible banner. Tab guards
  key off `rows.isEmpty`, never `status != ready`.

## Data source
- Unlike the Amber app, this one does NOT call an API. The user downloads a
  usage CSV from the Momentum Energy MyAccount Portal and uploads it in the
  app. The CSV is parsed and aggregated LOCALLY (in-browser / on-device); the
  file is never uploaded.
- Parsing uses the `csv` package (`CsvToListConverter`). The test fixture is
  `assets/Your_Usage_List_Sample.csv`.
- Source rows are 5-minute intervals (`METER_INTERVAL = 5`).
- Momentum's export occasionally carries a stray error-message row.
  `CsvState._parse` drops any row failing a shape check (>= 2 fields, a
  numeric kWh, a >= 14-char timestamp) before any date is parsed; fewer than
  two survivors is a clean parse failure, not a `RangeError`.
- Meter detection lives in `DataAggregator.detectNumMeters` only, called
  once per file by `CsvState`.

## Tests
- `test/bar_chart_test.dart` - `DataAggregator` aggregation tests. The ''1 Day''
  test asserts 48 half-hour bars and that each bar sums 6 x 5-minute values
  (e.g. 0.018 + 0.015 + ... + 0.019).
- `test/csv_state_test.dart` - meter/date detection, junk-row filtering, and
  the `error` vs `importError` split.
- Real widget suites per screen: `home_shell_test`, `data_tab_test`,
  `history_tab_test`, `settings_test`, `onboarding_test`,
  `widget_smoke_test`; shared fixtures in `test/test_data.dart`.
- `test/widget_test.dart` (the commented-out counter tombstone) is DELETED.
  Do not recreate it.

## Chart aggregation model (IMPORTANT)
- Graph bars are FIXED **half-hour buckets**: 2 bars/hour = 48 bars/day. This
  is intentional and must NOT be changed to one-bar-per-interval.
- 5-minute data: **6 five-minute intervals are summed into each half-hour
  bar** (value = sum of 6 x 5-min kWh or cost).
- In `lib/bar_chart.dart`, `graphPos` uses
  `date.hour * 2 + date.minute ~/ 30` (half-hour width). This is correct; do
  NOT "fix" it to a per-interval formula (that would create 288 bars and
  produce wrong time ranges and values).
- The daily supply charge is split per interval
  (`DAILY / 24 / (60 / METER_INTERVAL)`) and summed back to a half-hour amount
  across the 6 intervals in a bar.

## Key constants (lib/bar_chart.dart)
- `METER_INTERVAL` = 5 (the 5-minute source interval).
- `DAILY` = daily supply charge; `CONTROLLED`, `OFFPEAK`, `SHOULDER`, `PEAK` =
  tiered rates used by `_getCost`; `colors` = bar segment colours.
- Tariff/time-of-use bands in `_getCost`/`_getCostColor` are keyed on `graphPos`
  in half-hour units (e.g. `graphPos < 7 * 2` = before 07:00).

## Versioning
- `pubspec.yaml`: `version: x.y.z+build` (e.g. `1.3.3+22`).
- **Bump `appVersion` in `lib/version.dart` in the same commit** - the
  Settings > About screen shows it, and the two drift silently otherwise.

## Deployment
- No local deploy script. CI is Codemagic. **Push to `origin/master`** to
  trigger the build/deploy (web, Android, iOS). Still configured via the
  Codemagic dashboard's Workflow Editor; the committed `codemagic.yaml` is
  inert documentation of that workflow until the app is switched to YAML.
- Feature commits carry `[skip ci]`, so a **merge commit without `[skip ci]`
  triggers a release build**. Fast-forward, or make that merge a deliberate
  release.

## Gotchas
- Test runs can fail with a locked build dir (`build\unit_test_assets`). Fix
  by deleting the build folder (`Remove-Item -Recurse -Force build`) and
  re-running `flutter test`.
- Line endings are normalized via `.gitattributes` (`* text=auto`) since
  Aug 2026: the repo stores LF; Windows checkouts are CRLF. No need to
  hand-craft CRLF sequences in edits any more.
- A plain `git push origin master` can falsely print "Everything up-to-date"
  even when the commit is still local. Use `git push origin master --verbose`
  to force a real push, then confirm with `git log origin/master`.
- `lib/utils.dart` `darken`/`lighten` may still use the deprecated
  `Color.red/green/blue/alpha` getters (SDK deprecation, not a logic bug).
- **iOS / CocoaPods:** `ios/Podfile.lock` is NOT committed (only the lock in
  `pubspec.lock` is), so after any dependency bump that shifts the native pod
  graph, the Codemagic macOS agent's cached `Pods/` can fall out of sync with
  the freshly generated `Podfile.lock` and the build fails with "The sandbox is
  not in sync with the Podfile.lock. Run 'pod install'...". This is a CI cache
  issue, NOT an app-code bug. Fix by re-running the build (fresh worker) or
  clearing the pod cache first (`rm -rf ios/Podfile.lock ios/Pods && cd ios &&
  pod install`). Do NOT try to "remove CocoaPods" to speed up the build - this
  app uses native iOS plugins (`url_launcher`, `file_picker`,
  `device_preview_plus`) that Flutter wires in via CocoaPods; removing it would
  break the iOS build. CI is dashboard-configured (no `codemagic.yaml`).
- The Codemagic **Post-clone script** that clears and regenerates the Pods
  sandbox lives at `scripts/codemagic_post_clone.sh`. Paste its contents into
  the Codemagic dashboard "Post-clone script" field (it runs `flutter pub get`
  then `rm -rf ios/Pods ios/Podfile.lock && cd ios && pod install`).
